[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mClassification: architectural.[0m This repo is a minimal CommonJS Node scaffold ([38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m, [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m, no React, no
  build tooling, no tests) — a React todo list introduces a whole new stack rather than changing an existing flow. So:
  clarifying questions, approaches, a sectioned design, a written spec, then a plan. No code until you approve.

  First fork, and it's the one that shapes everything else:

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether todos survive a page reload, and if so, where they live.

  - [1mIn-memory only[0m — buys the fastest path to a working UI; costs everything on refresh. Fine for a demo or a UI
    exercise. Cheap to upgrade to localStorage later.
  - [1mlocalStorage[0m — buys real persistence per browser with no server; costs a small serialization layer and no
    cross-device sync. Still purely a frontend app. Also cheap to change later, as long as state is kept behind a small
    storage interface.
  - [1mBackend API + database[0m — buys multi-device and multi-user; costs a server, a schema, an API contract, auth
    questions, and deployment. This is the one that's expensive to add or remove later, because it changes the app's
    architecture, not just a module.

  [1mMy recommendation: localStorage.[0m It gives the app the property people actually expect from a todo list (your todos are
  still there tomorrow) at nearly the cost of the in-memory version, and if state access goes through one small storage
  module, swapping in a backend later is a contained change rather than a rewrite.

